  "requirement": "The `habeas-refactor` skill MUST tell agents not to edit evidence files by hand\nto follow a move, and that renaming a function or editing moved text is not a\nmove and needs reassessment.",
  "determination": true,
  "rationale": "structural: `requirements.cli.refactor_skill.limits` has no structural obligations. It says the `habeas-refactor` skill MUST tell agents (1) not to edit evidence files by hand to follow a move, and (2) that renaming a function or editing moved text is not a move and needs reassessment. Both clauses describe what the skill text says. The text prescribes no library, algorithm, signature, type, field or delegation through a named interface. I checked for an alternative implementation that keeps the behavior but breaks a clause, and found none. The skill could put these statements in any section, such as `Gotchas` or `Workflow`, in any wording or order, and the requirement would still hold. Where the statements go is discretionary.\n\nThe review root is `assets/skills/habeas-refactor/SKILL.md`. It is the document that carries the content, and it is linked to the requirement by an Implements edge. It is the artifact itself, not code that needs separate structural evidence. Its `Gotchas` section (`section[Habeas%20refactor]/section[Gotchas]`) has four paragraphs, which is where such guidance would sit. The outline tools elide the paragraph bodies, so I did not check the wording against the two clauses.\n\nNo further implementation roots are needed. Whether the skill text actually says these things is a content question for the behavioral criterion, not a structural one.\n\nbehavioral: The requirement has three behavioral obligations. The `habeas-refactor` skill must tell agents (1) not to edit evidence files by hand to follow a move, (2) that renaming a function is not a move and needs reassessment, and (3) that editing moved text is not a move and needs reassessment.\n\n`tests/authoring_cli.rs#fn[the_refactor_skill_never_hand_edits_evidence_and_a_rename_is_not_a_move]` has a `{test}` marker for the requirement. It extracts the `## Gotchas` section of the bundled `habeas-refactor` `SKILL.md` with `skill_md` and `section`. It then uses `assert_text`, which asserts `text.contains(phrase)` for each phrase.\n\nThe phrases map to the obligations:\n- Obligation 1: \"Never edit evidence files by hand to follow a move\".\n- Obligation 2: \"A rename is not a move. Renaming a function, type, or other item changes its code\" and \"its evidence needs reassessment\".\n- Obligation 3: \"Editing moved code or text in the same step leaves every record that read it stale\".\n\nThe assertions check the skill's actual text, so a skill that dropped or reversed any of these statements would fail the test. I found no unchecked obligation and no marking gap.",
  "references": "c99c8b54cda1189f7dc51e39c196d94fd3eecfc565b09694f3400654f095704d",
